<Project>
	<!--
	Local development packages: Nuget.Config makes $(PackageRepoFolder) - the shared repos\Packages
	feed - a package source, each build republishes into it, and the eviction target below stops a
	consumer's restore serving a stale copy of a version just rebuilt.

	Imported by exactly the two projects that pack, BaseApp.BlazorUI and BaseApp.ViewModels, which is
	why the version tool is wired HERE rather than in Solution.Build.props: that file is imported by
	five projects, the other three of which publish nothing.

	The timestamp-suffix scheme the old header described is gone, and so is the ConfigurePackageVersion
	target that served it. It set PackageVersion = Version at BeforeTargets="GenerateNuspec", which the
	SDK already does at evaluation (NuGet.Build.Tasks.Pack.targets defaults PackageVersion to Version),
	and it did so AFTER the eviction target had read PackageVersion - so under a tool that sets only
	Version the two disagreed, evicting one version while packing another. Its remaining Message tasks
	printed $(PkgSuffix) and $(UseTimestampSuffix), which nothing sets: Solution.Build.props derives
	PkgSuffix from a System.Build.props that does not exist in this workspace.
	-->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>true</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!--
	  DERIVED VERSIONING. Replaces the <Version>1.0.1</Version> literal Solution.Build.props used to
	  carry; the version now comes from version.json plus the number of commits since that file last
	  changed.

	  versionHeightOffset is 1 because the published version is 1.0.1: height 1 alone would recompute
	  1.0.1 and collide with what is already in the feed, so the first derived build lands at 1.0.2.
	  versionHeightOffsetAppliesTo retires the offset at the next version bump - without it a later move
	  to 1.1 would yield 1.1.1 rather than 1.1.0.

	  publicReleaseRefSpec names BOTH main and dev. Unlike repos/Service, which publishes nothing to a
	  registry and so needs only dev, this repo is pushed to GitHub Packages by
	  .github/workflows/publish-nuget.yaml on main while day-to-day work happens on dev. A branch left
	  out of that list still builds, but its version carries a -g<commithash> discriminator and is
	  therefore a PRERELEASE - which would quietly turn every locally built package into one, and every
	  consumer pin into a prerelease pin.

	  GitVersionBaseDirectory pins the height to the repo root so both projects compute ONE version. Not
	  per-project pathFilters: pack asks each referenced project for its own PackageVersion, so a commit
	  touching BaseApp.ViewModels alone would move the dependency recorded in BaseApp.BlazorUI's nuspec
	  while leaving BaseApp.BlazorUI's own version alone, and one version would then name two different
	  packages.
	-->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<GitVersionBaseDirectory>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)</GitVersionBaseDirectory>
	</PropertyGroup>
	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="Nerdbank.GitVersioning" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!--
	  A SHALLOW clone is loud - NBGV throws MSB4018. NO git repository at all is not: a tree with
	  version.json and no .git builds successfully, with no warning, at height 0 - a prerelease that
	  sorts BELOW the released version. Refuse it rather than ship it.

	  The shallow case is why publish-nuget.yaml now pins fetch-depth: 0. actions/checkout defaults to
	  depth 1, so without that change the first CI build after this file landed would have failed.
	-->
	<Target Name="RefuseAVersionWithNoGitHistory"
	        BeforeTargets="GetBuildVersion;Build"
	        Condition="!Exists('$(GitVersionBaseDirectory).git')">
		<Error Text="No git repository at $(GitVersionBaseDirectory), so the version cannot be derived. Nerdbank.GitVersioning does not fail here - it would quietly produce a height-0 prerelease that sorts BELOW the released version, and every package this build produced would carry it. Build from a full clone." />
	</Target>

	<!-- Create the Packages folder if it doesn't exist -->
	<Target Name="CreateDirectories" BeforeTargets="Build">
		<MakeDir Directories="$(PackageRepoFolder)"/>
	</Target>

	<!-- Copy Package Target -->
	<Target Name="CopyPackage" AfterTargets="Pack">
		<PropertyGroup>
			<!-- Pack names its output <PackageId>.<normalized PackageVersion>, which is NOT
			     $(AssemblyName).$(Version): PackageId need not equal AssemblyName, build metadata
			     (+g1a2b3c) is dropped from the file name though not from $(Version), a fourth part of .0
			     is dropped, and a two-part version is padded to three. Each of those made the old
			     hardcoded Copy fail with MSB3030, leaving the feed holding the PREVIOUS version and
			     never receiving the one just built. The rules are applied HERE, in a target that runs
			     after Pack, because that is when a version tool has had its say.

			     This reconstructs pack's name rather than reading it, so it models NuGet's normalization
			     and can be incomplete - leading zeros (1.0.010 -> 1.0.10) are NOT modelled. That is why
			     a miss is an Error and not a warning: nothing in this build gates on warnings, so a
			     silent miss would leave every consumer restoring stale bytes.

			     Why not @(NuGetPackOutput), which looks authoritative: it is computed by
			     _GetOutputItemsFromPack from $(PackageId)/$(PackageVersion) and its only
			     DependsOnTargets is _GetAbsoluteOutputPathsForPack, so it is a PREDICTION rather than a
			     record of what pack wrote, and whether it is right depends on which hook the version
			     tool uses. Reconstructing here does not depend on that choice, and unlike the item it is
			     checked against the disk.

			     $(PackageOutputPath) rather than a hardcoded bin\$(Configuration)\, so `dotnet pack -o`
			     no longer breaks the copy. -->
			<_PkgId>$(PackageId)</_PkgId>
			<_PkgId Condition=" '$(_PkgId)' == '' ">$(AssemblyName)</_PkgId>
			<_PkgVersion>$(PackageVersion)</_PkgVersion>
			<_PkgVersion Condition=" '$(_PkgVersion)' == '' ">$(Version)</_PkgVersion>
			<_PkgVersion>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Replace('$(_PkgVersion)', '[+].*$', ''))</_PkgVersion>
			<_PkgVersion>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Replace('$(_PkgVersion)', '^([0-9]+[.][0-9]+)(?=$|[-])', '$1.0'))</_PkgVersion>
			<_PkgVersion>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Replace('$(_PkgVersion)', '^([0-9]+[.][0-9]+[.][0-9]+)[.]0(?=$|-)', '$1'))</_PkgVersion>
			<_PkgDir>$(PackageOutputPath)</_PkgDir>
			<_PkgDir Condition=" '$(_PkgDir)' == '' ">$(BaseOutputPath)$(Configuration)\</_PkgDir>
			<_PkgDir>$([MSBuild]::EnsureTrailingSlash('$(_PkgDir)'))</_PkgDir>
		</PropertyGroup>
		<ItemGroup>
			<_ProducedPackages Include="$(_PkgDir)$(_PkgId).$(_PkgVersion).nupkg" Condition="Exists('$(_PkgDir)$(_PkgId).$(_PkgVersion).nupkg')" />
			<_ProducedPackages Include="$(_PkgDir)$(_PkgId).$(_PkgVersion).snupkg" Condition="Exists('$(_PkgDir)$(_PkgId).$(_PkgVersion).snupkg')" />
		</ItemGroup>
		<Error Condition="'@(_ProducedPackages)' == '' AND '$(IsPackable)' != 'false'" Text="Pack produced no $(_PkgId).$(_PkgVersion).nupkg in $(_PkgDir), so nothing was published to $(PackageRepoFolder). The name above is this target's reconstruction of pack's: PackageId, then PackageVersion with build metadata stripped, a two-part version padded to three, and a fourth part of .0 dropped. NuGet normalizes leading zeros too, which this does not model - if that is what differs, look at the file pack actually wrote in $(_PkgDir)." />
		<Message Condition="'@(_ProducedPackages)' != ''" Importance="high" Text="Copying @(_ProducedPackages->'%(Filename)%(Extension)', ', ') to $(PackageRepoFolder)" />
		<MakeDir Condition="'@(_ProducedPackages)' != ''" Directories="$(PackageRepoFolder)" />
		<Copy Condition="'@(_ProducedPackages)' != ''" SourceFiles="@(_ProducedPackages)" DestinationFolder="$(PackageRepoFolder)" />
	</Target>

	<!-- Once the new package is in the feed, remove this id's SUPERSEDED packages from BOTH the feed and
	     the build output directory, so each holds one version per id. It runs after CopyPackage on
	     purpose: if the copy fails, the previous version stays in the feed instead of leaving the id
	     with nothing. The files just produced are excluded in both places, so a same-version rebuild -
	     the ordinary edit-and-build loop - deletes nothing, and this can never remove what the build is
	     about to publish. The target does not run at all when pack produced nothing, so a feed can never
	     be emptied for an id this build did not replace.

	     THIS IS NEW WORK, not a port of the old target. The previous CleanExistingPackages ran at
	     BeforeTargets="Pack" over a bare "$(AssemblyName).*.nupkg" glob: under a FIXED version that
	     merely deleted the file about to be rewritten, but under a derived version every commit produces
	     a new file name, and nothing would otherwise stop either directory growing without bound.

	     ANCHORED ON THE VERSION, deliberately. That bare glob also matches every package whose id merely
	     EXTENDS this one - "BaseApp.ViewModels.*" would sweep a future BaseApp.ViewModels.Extras - so
	     the glob only narrows the candidates and a regex then requires "<id>." followed by a digit-led
	     version. The regexes use character classes and never a backslash: MSBuild on Linux and macOS
	     rewrites literal backslashes in property values to slashes, and CI here runs ubuntu-latest. -->
	<Target Name="CleanExistingPackages" AfterTargets="CopyPackage" Condition="'@(_ProducedPackages)' != ''">
		<PropertyGroup>
			<_CleanId>$(PackageId)</_CleanId>
			<_CleanId Condition=" '$(_CleanId)' == '' ">$(AssemblyName)</_CleanId>
			<_OwnPackagePattern>(?i)^$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Escape('$(_CleanId)'))[.][0-9]+([.][0-9]+){1,3}(-[0-9a-z][0-9a-z.-]*)?([+][0-9a-z][0-9a-z.-]*)?[.]s?nupkg$</_OwnPackagePattern>
		</PropertyGroup>
		<ItemGroup>
			<_FeedCandidates Include="$(PackageRepoFolder)\$(_CleanId).*.nupkg;$(PackageRepoFolder)\$(_CleanId).*.snupkg;$(_PkgDir)$(_CleanId).*.nupkg;$(_PkgDir)$(_CleanId).*.snupkg"
			                 Exclude="@(_ProducedPackages);@(_ProducedPackages->'$(PackageRepoFolder)\%(Filename)%(Extension)')" />
			<_OwnPackages Include="@(_FeedCandidates)" Condition="$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch('%(_FeedCandidates.Filename)%(_FeedCandidates.Extension)', '$(_OwnPackagePattern)'))" />
		</ItemGroup>
		<Message Condition="'@(_OwnPackages)' != ''" Importance="high" Text="Removing superseded $(_CleanId) packages: @(_OwnPackages->'%(Filename)%(Extension)', ', ')" />
		<!-- A superseded file another process still has open is not worth failing the build over; it is
		     swept on the next build. -->
		<Delete Files="@(_OwnPackages)" TreatErrorsAsWarnings="true" Retries="2" RetryDelayMilliseconds="250" />
	</Target>

	<!-- Evict this project's package from the NuGet global-packages folder after every build, so the
	     next restore in a consumer re-extracts it from the feed instead of serving the cached copy. A
	     same-version repack is the ORDINARY case, not a legacy one: a derived version is a function of
	     git state, and the edit-and-rebuild loop does not advance the commit, so it recomputes the same
	     version and rewrites the same package with different bytes. Without eviction a consumer keeps
	     building against the stale bytes.

	     DeleteSpecificPackage now runs in THIS project instance (DependsOnTargets), never through the
	     child <MSBuild> call it used to use. That child call re-evaluated the project from scratch,
	     where $(Version) was the STATIC value: a version tool sets the version inside a target, which a
	     child evaluation never runs, so with this change in place it would have evicted <id>/1.0.0 - the
	     SDK default, now that Solution.Build.props carries no literal - while never touching the version
	     actually built.

	     SKIPPED IN THE CROSS-TARGETING OUTER BUILD. A project with <TargetFrameworks> (plural) builds in
	     two passes, and the outer pass runs none of the version tool's targets, so there $(Version) is
	     whatever static evaluation left - the SDK default. Neither project here is cross-targeting today
	     (Solution.Build.props sets a single <TargetFramework>), so this guard is insurance against one
	     becoming so later. IsCrossTargetingBuild is set, with this exact condition, by both
	     Microsoft.NET.Sdk's Sdk.targets and MSBuild's Microsoft.Managed.Before.targets; a target
	     Condition is evaluated when the target RUNS, so it sees that value even though this file is
	     imported before the SDK's targets. -->
	<Target Name="DeletePackage" AfterTargets="Build" DependsOnTargets="DeleteSpecificPackage"
	        Condition="'$(IsCrossTargetingBuild)' != 'true'" />
	<Target Name="DeleteSpecificPackage">
		<PropertyGroup>
			<!-- Evict from the global-packages folder the restore ACTUALLY used, not a hardcoded
			     ~/.nuget/packages. $(NuGetPackageRoot) comes from the restore-generated nuget.g.props
			     and follows the per-workspace globalPackagesFolder set in the workspace Nuget.Config.
			     NuGet writes a RELOCATED root WITHOUT a trailing separator (the default has one), so it
			     is ensured, or the path collapses to "...\.nuget-packagesbaseapp.viewmodels\1.0.1" and
			     eviction silently no-ops (seen 2026-09-02). Falls back to the NuGet default only when no
			     restore has run. -->
			<_PackageRoot>$(NuGetPackageRoot)</_PackageRoot>
			<_PackageRoot Condition=" '$(_PackageRoot)' == '' AND '$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT' ">$(UserProfile)\.nuget\packages\</_PackageRoot>
			<_PackageRoot Condition=" '$(_PackageRoot)' == '' AND '$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT' ">$(Home)/.nuget/packages/</_PackageRoot>
			<_PackageRoot>$([MSBuild]::EnsureTrailingSlash('$(_PackageRoot)'))</_PackageRoot>
			<!-- The id and version the package was ACTUALLY built with, read here after the version tool
			     has run: PackageVersion is what pack stamps, Version is its default. NuGet names the
			     cache folder by the lower-cased id and the NORMALIZED, lower-cased version - build
			     metadata (+g1a2b3c) is not part of it and a fourth part of 0 is dropped - so the same
			     normalization is applied here. -->
			<_EvictId>$(PackageId)</_EvictId>
			<_EvictId Condition=" '$(_EvictId)' == '' ">$(AssemblyName)</_EvictId>
			<_EvictVersion>$(PackageVersion)</_EvictVersion>
			<_EvictVersion Condition=" '$(_EvictVersion)' == '' ">$(Version)</_EvictVersion>
			<_EvictVersion>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Replace('$(_EvictVersion)', '[+].*$', ''))</_EvictVersion>
			<_EvictVersion>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Replace('$(_EvictVersion)', '^([0-9]+[.][0-9]+)(?=$|[-])', '$1.0'))</_EvictVersion>
			<_EvictVersion>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Replace('$(_EvictVersion)', '^([0-9]+[.][0-9]+[.][0-9]+)[.]0(?=$|-)', '$1'))</_EvictVersion>
			<_EvictVersion>$(_EvictVersion.ToLowerInvariant())</_EvictVersion>
			<PackageCacheFolder Condition=" '$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT' ">$(_PackageRoot)$(_EvictId.ToLowerInvariant())\$(_EvictVersion)</PackageCacheFolder>
			<PackageCacheFolder Condition=" '$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT' ">$(_PackageRoot)$(_EvictId.ToLowerInvariant())/$(_EvictVersion)</PackageCacheFolder>
		</PropertyGroup>
		<!-- Three honest outcomes, decided by STATE rather than by exit code. On Windows `rmdir /s /q`
		     exits 0 when a file inside is in use and it deleted only what it could, so an exit-code test
		     reports that as success. Exists() in a task condition is evaluated when the task runs, after
		     the Exec, and sees the post-deletion state. (The target this replaces printed "Failed to
		     delete package. Error code: " on the NOTHING-TO-EVICT path: the Exec never ran, the exit
		     code stayed empty, and '' != '0' is true. That noise is what the /build skill documents as
		     safe to ignore; it is now gone rather than documented.) -->
		<!-- A destructive delete NEVER runs on a version we could not determine. With $(_EvictVersion)
		     empty the path collapses to the id root and rmdir would take EVERY cached version of the
		     package. Nothing upstream should ever produce an empty version - the SDK defaults it, and
		     the two fallbacks above cover an unset PackageId - so this is a bug detector, not a fallback
		     path, and it warns rather than passing silently. -->
		<Warning Condition=" '$(_EvictVersion)' == '' " Text="No version could be determined for $(_EvictId); skipping the package-cache eviction rather than deleting every cached version of it. The build is producing a package whose version this target cannot see - check version.json and any &lt;Version&gt; override." />
		<Message Condition=" '$(_EvictVersion)' != '' AND !Exists('$(PackageCacheFolder)')" Importance="high" Text="No cached $(_EvictId) $(_EvictVersion) under $(_PackageRoot) - nothing to evict." />
		<Exec Condition=" '$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT' AND '$(_EvictVersion)' != '' AND Exists('$(PackageCacheFolder)')"
			  Command="cmd.exe /c &quot;if exist &quot;$(PackageCacheFolder)&quot; rmdir /s /q &quot;$(PackageCacheFolder)&quot;&quot;"
			  ContinueOnError="true">
			<Output TaskParameter="ExitCode" PropertyName="_EvictExitCode"/>
		</Exec>
		<Exec Condition=" '$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT' AND '$(_EvictVersion)' != '' AND Exists('$(PackageCacheFolder)')"
			  Command="rm -rf &quot;$(PackageCacheFolder)&quot;"
			  ContinueOnError="true">
			<Output TaskParameter="ExitCode" PropertyName="_EvictExitCode"/>
		</Exec>
		<Message Condition="'$(_EvictExitCode)' != '' AND !Exists('$(PackageCacheFolder)')" Importance="high" Text="Evicted $(PackageCacheFolder); the next restore re-extracts $(_EvictId) $(_EvictVersion) from the feed." />
		<Warning Condition="'$(_EvictExitCode)' != '' AND Exists('$(PackageCacheFolder)')" Text="Could not evict $(PackageCacheFolder) (exit $(_EvictExitCode); a file inside is probably in use) - a consumer may restore a STALE $(_EvictId) $(_EvictVersion). Close whatever holds it and rebuild." />
	</Target>

	<!-- =====================================================================================
	     SIBLING DEPENDENCIES PACK AS EXACT RANGES, NOT MINIMUMS.

	     BaseApp.BlazorUI depends on BaseApp.ViewModels through a ProjectReference. Pack renders such a
	     dependency from the referenced project's $(PackageVersion) as a BARE version, which NuGet reads
	     as "[x, )". These two are built from one commit and versioned together, so a consumer pinning
	     BaseApp.BlazorUI must not silently resolve a later BaseApp.ViewModels - and under derived
	     versioning "later" now arrives on every commit rather than never.

	     CPM CANNOT REACH IT - Directory.Packages.props governs PackageReference, and a ProjectReference
	     never passes through it. Measured on SDK 10.0.301: Version, VersionOverride and ExactVersion
	     metadata on the ProjectReference item are silently IGNORED, and giving the referenced project a
	     bracketed $(PackageVersion) fails the pack outright, because a package version cannot be a range.

	     WHERE THIS HOOKS. GenerateNuspec depends on _GetProjectReferenceVersions, which collects
	     @(_ProjectReferencesWithVersions) with ProjectVersion metadata before PackTask turns those items
	     into the nuspec <dependency> entries. Editing the metadata in between is an item transform at a
	     real seam, not a rewrite of generated XML.

	     THE RISK, AND THAT IT IS UNGUARDED HERE. @(_ProjectReferencesWithVersions) is SDK-INTERNAL - the
	     underscore says so - so a future SDK could rename it and this target would silently stop
	     applying. That is a FAIL-OPEN: the build still succeeds and the package still publishes, just
	     with an open-ended dependency. Scutara's equivalent is backed by a publish workflow that refuses
	     any package whose sibling dependency is not an exact range; this repo's
	     .github/workflows/publish-nuget.yaml has no such check, so nothing here would catch it. Until it
	     does, verify by hand after an SDK upgrade: unzip a BaseApp.BlazorUI .nupkg and confirm the
	     BaseApp.ViewModels <dependency> version reads "[x]" and not "x".
	     ===================================================================================== -->
	<Target Name="BracketSiblingProjectReferences" AfterTargets="_GetProjectReferenceVersions">
		<ItemGroup>
			<_ProjectReferencesWithVersions Update="@(_ProjectReferencesWithVersions)"
					Condition="!$([System.String]::Copy('%(ProjectVersion)').StartsWith('['))">
				<ProjectVersion>[%(ProjectVersion)]</ProjectVersion>
			</_ProjectReferencesWithVersions>
		</ItemGroup>
	</Target>

</Project>
